7,12,19,20,92,481,1124,37018,90,17,15170,87,61,17,12,16,15,87,12,21,24,92,284,220,15,59,701,582,1191,553,20194,17767,88,284,856,61,17,481,220,16,15,87,481,220,21,24,59,568,1096,48968,15491,9606,279,23606,438,11017,24391,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,2610,1231,312,18883,55856,279,3491,323,18130,279,6291,504,279,7167,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind the positive solution to\n\\[\\frac 1{x^2-10x-29}+\\frac1{x^2-10x-45}-\\frac 2{x^2-10x-69}=0.\\]\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo solve the equation \\(\\frac{1}{x^2-10x-29} + \\frac{1}{x^2-10x-45} - \\frac{2}{x^2-10x-69} = 0\\), we start by letting \\(y = x^2 - 10x - 69\\). This substitution simplifies the equation as follows:\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"You may re-analyze the problem and rewrite the solution from the beginning.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"d93b24e438fc438fcde9a054b5affd10303bb231c75b01d3e34f3bcbaa9ba149"},"seconds":48.634917974472046,"seed":1008438626788715234,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the equation \n\\[\n\\frac{1}{x^2-10x-29} + \\frac{1}{x^2-10x-45} - \\frac{2}{x^2-10x-69} = 0,\n\\]\nwe will start by making a substitution to simplify the equation. Let \n\\[\ny = x^2 - 10x - 69.\n\\]\nThen, we can express the terms in the equation in terms of \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\nx^2 - 10x - 29 = y + 40,\n\\]\n\\[\nx^2 - 10x - 45 = y + 24,\n\\]\n\\[\nx^2 - 10x - 69 = y.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these into the original equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{y+40} + \\frac{1}{y+24} - \\frac{2}{y} = 0.\n\\]\n\nNext, we find a common denominator for the fractions:\n\\[\n\\frac{y(y+24) + y(y+40) - 2(y+40)(y+24)}{y(y+40)(y+24)} = 0.\n\\]\n\nSince the numerator must be zero for the fraction to be zero, we set the numerator equal to zero:\n\\[\ny(y+24) + y(y+40) - 2(y+40)(y+24) = 0.\n\\]\n\nExpanding each term:\n\\[\ny^2 + 24y + y^2 + 40y - 2(y^2 + 64y + 960) = 0,\n\\]\n\\[\n2y^2 + 64y - 2y^2 - 128y - 1920 = 0,\n\\]\n\\[\n-64y - 1920 = 0.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(y\\):\n\\[\n-64y = 1920,\n\\]\n\\[\ny = -30.\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute back \\(y = x^2 - 10x - 69\\):\n\\[\nx^2 - 10x - 69 = -30,\n\\]\n\\[\nx^2 - 10x - 39 = 0.\n\\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -10\\), and \\(c = -39\\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 \\pm \\sqrt{(-10)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-39)}}{2 \\cdot 1},\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 \\pm \\sqrt{100 + 156}}{2},\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 \\pm \\sqrt{256}}{2},\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 \\pm 16}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{26}{2} = 13,\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-6}{2} = -3.\n\\]\n\nSince we are asked for the positive solution, we have:\n\\[\n\\boxed{13}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{13}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To solve the equation \n\\[\n\\frac{1}{x^2-10x-29} + \\frac{1}{x^2-10x-45} - \\frac{2}{x^2-10x-69} = 0,\n\\]\nwe will start by making a substitution to simplify the equation. Let \n\\[\ny = x^2 - 10x - 69.\n\\]\nThen, we can express the terms in the equation in terms of \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\nx^2 - 10x - 29 = y + 40,\n\\]\n\\[\nx^2 - 10x - 45 = y + 24,\n\\]\n\\[\nx^2 - 10x - 69 = y.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these into the original equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{y+40} + \\frac{1}{y+24} - \\frac{2}{y} = 0.\n\\]\n\nNext, we find a common denominator for the fractions:\n\\[\n\\frac{y(y+24) + y(y+40) - 2(y+40)(y+24)}{y(y+40)(y+24)} = 0.\n\\]\n\nSince the numerator must be zero for the fraction to be zero, we set the numerator equal to zero:\n\\[\ny(y+24) + y(y+40) - 2(y+40)(y+24) = 0.\n\\]\n\nExpanding each term:\n\\[\ny^2 + 24y + y^2 + 40y - 2(y^2 + 64y + 960) = 0,\n\\]\n\\[\n2y^2 + 64y - 2y^2 - 128y - 1920 = 0,\n\\]\n\\[\n-64y - 1920 = 0.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(y\\):\n\\[\n-64y = 1920,\n\\]\n\\[\ny = -30.\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute back \\(y = x^2 - 10x - 69\\):\n\\[\nx^2 - 10x - 69 = -30,\n\\]\n\\[\nx^2 - 10x - 39 = 0.\n\\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -10\\), and \\(c = -39\\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 \\pm \\sqrt{(-10)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-39)}}{2 \\cdot 1},\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 \\pm \\sqrt{100 + 156}}{2},\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 \\pm \\sqrt{256}}{2},\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 \\pm 16}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{26}{2} = 13,\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-6}{2} = -3.\n\\]\n\nSince we are asked for the positive solution, we have:\n\\[\n\\boxed{13}.\n\\]","id":"intermediate_algebra:571"}
